# Reconstruction scope and claims

This rule belongs in a dedicated reconstruction project. Apply it to reconstruction work and its artifacts.

- Record target version, replacement interfaces, environment, accepted requirements, exclusions, and authorized inputs before implementation.
- Classify a run as black-box, documentation-based, or source-informed according to actual exposure. Preserve mixed lineage per artifact.
- Distinguish functional conformance, information-flow controls, provenance, similarity, and legal review. Each needs its own evidence.
- Report observed, documented, inferred, and unknown behaviors separately.
- Bound compatibility claims to accepted scope and exercised conditions. Compiling establishes a build result.
- Never infer legal clearance, absence of infringement, or license freedom from separate agents, different syntax/language, passing tests, or a similarity score.

Example: "Matched the 36 accepted serialization cases on Linux/Python 3.12; concurrent writers remain unobserved."
